      },
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00330:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "The production of adenosine triphosphate and water from fats, carbohydrates and proteins involves four stages. Acetyl coenzyme A formed in second stage undergoes citric acid cycle and electron-transport chain."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_29_01_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00002",
      "segmentId": "m00330:para:para-00002"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00330:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "The structure of acetyl Co A, a thioester. It comprises of a C H 3 C O linked to a phosphopantetheinyl adenosine bisphosphate."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_29_01_002.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00003",
      "segmentId": "m00330:para:para-00003"
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00004",
      "segmentId": "m00330:para:para-00004"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00003",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00330:alt:fig-00003-alt",
          "text": "The reversible reaction of adenosine diphosphate with phosphate and hydrogen ions. The removal of water leads to the product named adenosine triphosphate. Diphosphate and triphosphate groups in structures are labeled."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_29_01_003.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00005",
      "segmentId": "m00330:para:para-00005"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00004",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00330:alt:fig-00004-alt",
          "text": "The reaction shows an alcohol reacting with adenosine triphosphate to form an intermediate, which decomposes to form a phosphate ester, adenosine diphosphate, and magnesium cation."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_29_01_006.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00006",
      "segmentId": "m00330:para:para-00006"
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00007",
      "segmentId": "m00330:para:para-00007"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00005",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00330:alt:fig-00005-alt",
          "text": "A reversible reaction where A reacts with m to form B and n. The value of delta G is greater than zero."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_29_01_007.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00008",
      "segmentId": "m00330:para:para-00008"
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00009",
      "segmentId": "m00330:para:para-00009"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00006",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00330:alt:fig-00006-alt",
          "text": "A reversible reaction where n reacts with o to form p and q. The value of delta G is much less than zero."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_29_01_008.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00010",
      "segmentId": "m00330:para:para-00010"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00007",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00330:alt:fig-00007-alt",
          "text": "Two reversible reactions are combined to give a net overall reversible reaction, A plus m plus give B plus p plus q. The delta G is less than zero."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_29_01_009.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00011",
      "segmentId": "m00330:para:para-00011"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00008",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00330:alt:fig-00008-alt",
          "text": "Glucose reacts reversibly with phosphate ion to give glucose-6-phosphate and water. The standard G value is equal to plus 13 point 8 kilojoules."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_29_01_010.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00012",
      "segmentId": "m00330:para:para-00012"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00009",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00330:alt:fig-00009-alt",
          "text": "Two reactions combine to form net reaction where glucose combines with adenosine triphosphate to give glucose-6-phosphate, adenosine diphosphate and hydrogen positive ion. The overall  delta G is less than zero."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_29_01_011.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
